{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially during downtrends. Apply a downward level shift (roughly 8–12 EUR) when recent momentum is negative.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model is slow to recognize regime shifts from rising to falling markets; it keeps predicting stabilization/rebound while the market continues to sell off. Increase weight on latest 5–10 days’ slope; if slope < 0, enforce continued decline for the next ~1–2 weeks.",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following a run-up/spike, the model projects renewed strength (often even higher highs) but outcomes mean-revert lower. After a 10–20 day rally or new local max, cap upside and impose mean-reversion toward the 20–30d average.",
    "Horizon-dependent error: forecast error grows with horizon—near-term is closer, but beyond ~7–10 days the model increasingly overestimates and underestimates downside. Apply a horizon-scaled downward adjustment that increases with lead time.",
    "Downside underestimation: magnitude of drawdowns is underpredicted (realized declines are steeper). When volatility rises and returns are negative, widen downside moves (e.g., amplify negative daily changes by ~1.2–1.5x) and reduce probability of quick rebound."
  ]
}
